1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,580
There is only one thing in the entire

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,960
universe you can be absolutely sure

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,555
exists.

4
00:00:05,905 --> 00:00:07,803
Not the chair you're sitting on.

5
00:00:08,153 --> 00:00:09,924
Not the people you love.

6
00:00:10,274 --> 00:00:12,599
Not even the screen in front of you.

7
00:00:12,949 --> 00:00:15,275
Just one thing: your own mind.

8
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:18,436
Everything else — every face, every voice,

9
00:00:18,436 --> 00:00:21,123
every memory of a world outside — could,

10
00:00:21,123 --> 00:00:23,747
in principle, be something your mind is

11
00:00:23,747 --> 00:00:25,246
producing on its own.

12
00:00:25,596 --> 00:00:28,177
This idea is called solipsism.

13
00:00:28,527 --> 00:00:30,678
And here's what makes it unsettling:

14
00:00:30,678 --> 00:00:33,105
for more than two thousand years, some of

15
00:00:33,105 --> 00:00:35,312
the greatest thinkers in history have

16
00:00:35,312 --> 00:00:36,911
tried to prove it wrong...

17
00:00:37,261 --> 00:00:39,528
...and they still argue about whether

18
00:00:39,528 --> 00:00:40,888
anyone has succeeded.

19
00:00:41,238 --> 00:00:43,353
By the end of this video, you'll know

20
00:00:43,353 --> 00:00:44,886
where this idea came from,

21
00:00:45,236 --> 00:00:47,286
why your brain spent years of your

22
00:00:47,286 --> 00:00:49,225
childhood learning to escape it,

23
00:00:49,575 --> 00:00:51,908
and why one famous logician openly

24
00:00:51,908 --> 00:00:53,799
admitted she believed it.

25
00:00:54,149 --> 00:00:55,943
Quick favor before we start:

26
00:00:55,943 --> 00:00:58,489
if you like ideas that break your brain a

27
00:00:58,489 --> 00:00:59,819
little, subscribe.

28
00:00:59,819 --> 00:01:01,787
It genuinely helps the channel.

29
00:01:02,137 --> 00:01:03,844
The word comes from Latin:

30
00:01:03,844 --> 00:01:06,198
solus, meaning "alone," and ipse,

31
00:01:06,198 --> 00:01:08,494
meaning "self." Alone with the self.

32
00:01:08,844 --> 00:01:11,228
Philosophers usually separate two

33
00:01:11,228 --> 00:01:12,023
versions.

34
00:01:12,373 --> 00:01:14,686
The strong version, metaphysical

35
00:01:14,686 --> 00:01:15,972
solipsism, says:

36
00:01:15,972 --> 00:01:17,450
only my mind exists.

37
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:20,278
That's it. The world and other people are

38
00:01:20,278 --> 00:01:22,463
just contents of my experience.

39
00:01:22,813 --> 00:01:25,293
The weaker version, epistemological

40
00:01:25,293 --> 00:01:27,835
solipsism, is more modest — and much

41
00:01:27,835 --> 00:01:29,107
harder to escape.

42
00:01:29,457 --> 00:01:31,912
It says: I can't know that anything

43
00:01:31,912 --> 00:01:33,508
outside my mind exists.

44
00:01:33,508 --> 00:01:35,963
Maybe it does. I just can't prove it.

45
00:01:36,313 --> 00:01:38,652
Most philosophers reject the first.

46
00:01:38,652 --> 00:01:41,115
The second is the one that keeps them

47
00:01:41,115 --> 00:01:42,223
awake at night.

48
00:01:42,573 --> 00:01:45,367
The idea is older than you might think.

49
00:01:45,717 --> 00:01:48,243
Around the fifth century BC, the Greek

50
00:01:48,243 --> 00:01:50,769
thinker Gorgias wrote a work usually

51
00:01:50,769 --> 00:01:52,309
called On Non-Being.

52
00:01:52,659 --> 00:01:54,801
Its argument came in three steps:

53
00:01:54,801 --> 00:01:57,477
nothing exists; even if something existed,

54
00:01:57,477 --> 00:02:00,154
it couldn't be known; and even if it could

55
00:02:00,154 --> 00:02:02,711
be known, it couldn't be communicated to

56
00:02:02,711 --> 00:02:03,603
anyone else.

57
00:02:03,953 --> 00:02:06,565
Whether Gorgias meant it seriously or as a

58
00:02:06,565 --> 00:02:09,002
showpiece of clever rhetoric, the third

59
00:02:09,002 --> 00:02:11,265
step points straight at our problem:

60
00:02:11,265 --> 00:02:13,703
your experience is locked inside you.

61
00:02:14,053 --> 00:02:16,302
In ancient China, the philosopher

62
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:18,426
Zhuangzi told a famous story.

63
00:02:18,776 --> 00:02:21,515
He dreamed he was a butterfly, fluttering

64
00:02:21,515 --> 00:02:24,067
happily, with no idea he was Zhuangzi.

65
00:02:24,417 --> 00:02:26,934
Then he woke up — and wasn't sure anymore.

66
00:02:27,284 --> 00:02:29,803
Was he a man who had dreamed of being a

67
00:02:29,803 --> 00:02:32,383
butterfly... or a butterfly now dreaming

68
00:02:32,383 --> 00:02:33,343
he was a man?

